          "id": "sec-1-1",
          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Theory: Design Basis and Project Definition",
          "content": "**Design objective:** Convert a commercial request into testable process specifications, engineering constraints and a transparent design basis.\n\n**Common teaching scenario:** A hypothetical aqueous formulation plant with a market forecast, two feedstocks and uncertain operating hours.\n\n## Unit-level engineering decision\n\nThe essential problem in this unit is not memorizing equations. It is forming a defensible decision from incomplete evidence. Convert a commercial request into testable process specifications, engineering constraints and a transparent design basis. The case studies are synthetic; they provide a transparent set of inputs that can be checked without access to an operating facility. Before a numerical conclusion is adopted, identify the required physical evidence, constraints, and operating modes. Make clear whether the outcome is a conceptual screening result, a preliminary design basis, a vendor-specified rating, or a compliance decision. The four lessons examine distinct aspects of this decision.\n\n## Structured lesson sequence\n\n### 1. [Design objectives and production capacity](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-01.html)\n\nProduction rate, annual demand and utilization must be on a consistent basis. **Governing equation:** `Annual saleable mass = nameplate rate \u00d7 scheduled hours \u00d7 availability \u00d7 saleable yield`. **Numerical benchmark:** If 2.5 t/h is scheduled for 7200 h, availability is 0.90 and yield is 0.94, product is 15228 t/y. **Decision question:** The decision to build additional capacity should reflect demand uncertainty and opportunity cost rather than maximizing a spreadsheet output.\n\n### 2. [Product specifications and feed variability](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-02.html)\n\nSpecifications describe measurable output properties and tolerance, not just a nominal composition. **Governing equation:** `w_A,product = (F1 w_A1 + F2 w_A2)/(F1+F2)`. **Numerical benchmark:** Mix 4 t/h with 10% solute and 6 t/h with 30% solute: the result is 22% solute, assuming compatible nonreacting streams. **Decision question:** Treat a quality target and a tolerance range as separate constraints and check whether uncertain feeds can satisfy both.\n\n### 3. [Time basis, operating modes and turndown](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-03.html)\n\nA plant sized only at the average hour can be infeasible at peak loading or during planned outages. **Governing equation:** `required design rate = annual requirement/(available hours \u00d7 production yield)`. **Numerical benchmark:** For 12000 t/y, 6500 running hours and 0.92 saleable yield, required gross rate is approximately 2.007 t/h. **Decision question:** Distinguish three decisions: nameplate sizing, buffer inventory and campaign sequencing.\n\n### 4. [Assumptions register and basis freeze](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-04.html)\n\nEvery inferred cost, feed property, utility limit and tolerance belongs in a revision-controlled assumption register. **Governing equation:** `design margin = installed design capacity / required duty \u2212 1`. **Numerical benchmark:** A heat duty of 400 kW with 20% deliberate margin produces a 480 kW specified rating before any vendor adjustment. **Decision question:** A design-basis freeze is a managed snapshot, not a statement that uncertain inputs became facts.\n\n## Integrated design activity\n\nConstruct a compact design dossier for the following system: A hypothetical aqueous formulation plant with a market forecast, two feedstocks and uncertain operating hours. The dossier must contain a flowsheet or decision diagram, a variable and unit register, an assumption register, at least two checks based on first principles, a sensitivity chart from one of the interactive models, a documented failure case, and an explicit decision with a reversal condition. Begin with the minimum information needed to formulate the problem; do not assign invented precise values to unknown vendor or market inputs. When data is unavailable, use a marked synthetic scenario and report the evidence gap.\n\n## Evidence and model boundaries\n\n**Topic 1 \u2014 Design objectives and production capacity.** Use a sensitivity table for availability and yield; do not conceal lost production inside the stated capacity. The next evidence sources are sales forecast, uptime logs, quality rejection rates and production accounting. Any screening estimate using `Annual saleable mass = nameplate rate \u00d7 scheduled hours \u00d7 availability \u00d7 saleable yield` remains conditional until those inputs are independently confirmed. A graph can make sensitivity visible; it cannot supply absent physical evidence.\n\n**Topic 2 \u2014 Product specifications and feed variability.** Audit mass fraction units and verify the mixed quality lies between the two inlet qualities. The next evidence sources are feed certificates of analysis, sampling intervals, uncertainty and specification method. Any screening estimate using `w_A,product = (F1 w_A1 + F2 w_A2)/(F1+F2)` remains conditional until those inputs are independently confirmed. A graph can make sensitivity visible; it cannot supply absent physical evidence.\n\n**Topic 3 \u2014 Time basis, operating modes and turndown.** A minimum stable operating rate may prohibit arbitrarily low turndown during off-peak periods. The next evidence sources are demand profile, maintenance schedule, changeover time and equipment turndown tests. Any screening estimate using `required design rate = annual requirement/(available hours \u00d7 production yield)` remains conditional until those inputs are independently confirmed. A graph can make sensitivity visible; it cannot supply absent physical evidence.\n\n**Topic 4 \u2014 Assumptions register and basis freeze.** Describe which uncertainties a margin covers; unspecified safety factors are not valid substitutes for hazard evaluation. The next evidence sources are approved project basis, vendor ranges, interface matrix and signed revision history. Any screening estimate using `design margin = installed design capacity / required duty \u2212 1` remains conditional until those inputs are independently confirmed. A graph can make sensitivity visible; it cannot supply absent physical evidence.\n\n## Unit laboratories\n\n- [Lab 01: Annual saleable capacity](../labs/lab-01.html) \u2014 Separate installed capacity from realized output and the impact of uptime.. Verification: At availability 0 or yield 0, saleable production is zero by the governing equation.\n\n- [Lab 02: Blend composition and component closure](../labs/lab-02.html) \u2014 Mix two nonreacting streams and audit solute conservation.. Verification: Mix composition must lie between feed compositions; solute balance residual must vanish.\n\n- [Lab 03: Required hourly capacity](../labs/lab-03.html) \u2014 Translate annual demand into operating-hour requirement.. Verification: Increasing running hours decreases the required nominal rate; all positive cases remain finite.\n\n## Unit review protocol\n\nFor each model, run default and altered cases. Check the governing relation algebraically for one point; verify dimensional consistency and domain validity; identify at least one limiting case; export a CSV and preserve the selected parameter settings in the report. A smooth graph can still be wrong, so treat independent calculations and physical evidence as separate gates. Before using the result outside a classroom, request suitable measured inputs, professional design reviews and applicable approvals.\n\n[16-question assessment](../assessments/unit-01.html) \u00b7 [Applied project](../projects/unit-01.html) \u00b7 [Book contents](../index.html)"
