          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Equipment Specifications and Preliminary Sizing",
          "content": "# Applied design project 4: Equipment Specifications and Preliminary Sizing\n\n**Scenario:** A tank, transfer pump, process heater and heat-exchanger candidate are provisionally sized for the conceptual flowsheet.\n\n**Engineering goal:** Use transparent first-cut calculations, keep cost scaling distinct from ratings and identify vendor-required information.\n\n## Project brief and boundary\n\nYou are a junior design team preparing a conceptual recommendation for a supervised technical review. The project is **not** a request to produce construction-ready specifications. It asks whether a candidate concept merits a funded next study based on traceable evidence. Make one primary design decision linked to equipment specifications and preliminary sizing and identify both how it could fail and what information is needed before capital is committed. Design responsibility, commercial feasibility, safety, permit requirements and site-specific code compliance remain independent gate reviews.\n\n## Task A \u2014 Form a reproducible design basis\n\n**A1. Tanks and buffer holdup:** Residence time and surge allowances determine initial working volume, not vessel mechanical design. Start from `V_work = Q_volumetric \u00d7 residence time` and reproduce the benchmark: A 20 m\u00b3/h feed and a 30-minute hold-up target require 10 m\u00b3 working liquid capacity. Label numbers as synthetic, experimental, quoted or specified. Identify the actual evidence source required: inlet variability, overflow protection, fluid properties and maintenance constraints.\n\n**A2. Heat exchanger preliminary area:** Estimate area from a defensible duty, overall U and endpoint temperature differences. Start from `A = Q/(U \u00d7 LMTD) for a feasible countercurrent terminal temperature profile` and reproduce the benchmark: At duty 250 kW, U=0.5 kW/(m\u00b2 K), and LMTD=25 K, area is 20 m\u00b2. Label numbers as synthetic, experimental, quoted or specified. Identify the actual evidence source required: service-side fouling resistances, phase behavior and vendor thermal-rating check.\n\n**A3. Pipes, pressure loss and pump energy:** Hydraulics connect diameter, velocity, friction and pump power to layout choices. Start from `\u0394P = f(L/D)(\u03c1 v\u00b2/2); P = \u0394P Q / \u03b7` and reproduce the benchmark: For a fixed roughness-independent f, halving diameter raises velocity fourfold at equal volumetric flow. Label numbers as synthetic, experimental, quoted or specified. Identify the actual evidence source required: fluid density, viscosity, operating envelope, line routing and pump curves.\n\n**A4. Design margins and vendor datasheets:** An engineering specification should distinguish estimated process duty from specified design rating. Start from `specified heat load = verified heat load \u00d7 (1+ documented allowance)` and reproduce the benchmark: A 300 kW calculated thermal load with 15% allowance yields a provisional 345 kW datasheet duty. Label numbers as synthetic, experimental, quoted or specified. Identify the actual evidence source required: applicable equipment code, corrosion data, design pressure and temperature, vendor quotes.\n\n## Task B \u2014 Build a traceable numerical investigation\n\n**[Lab 10 \u2014 Tank working volume from holdup](../labs/lab-10.html):** Find the difference between working liquid volume and total conceptual shell capacity. Validate the bound: Working volume must scale linearly with flow at constant residence time. Export the CSV and annotate which equation limits the model. Do not claim that an illustrative spreadsheet predicts a real plant.\n\n**[Lab 11 \u2014 Countercurrent LMTD and area](../labs/lab-11.html):** Explore the sudden area growth when a specified endpoint temperature approach becomes very small. Validate the bound: If either endpoint approach is nonpositive, declare profile infeasible instead of drawing false area. Export the CSV and annotate which equation limits the model. Do not claim that an illustrative spreadsheet predicts a real plant.\n\n**[Lab 12 \u2014 Darcy pressure loss and pump power](../labs/lab-12.html):** Compare required shaft power at diameter 0.10 versus 0.20 m under the same assumptions. Validate the bound: Pressure loss is nonnegative and scales with pipe length for fixed f and flow. Export the CSV and annotate which equation limits the model. Do not claim that an illustrative spreadsheet predicts a real plant.\n\n## Task C \u2014 Challenge the concept\n\nConstruct a short failure-first register with at least one physical failure, one commercial or cost failure, one operational difficulty and one missing-data risk. For equipment specifications and preliminary sizing, list the observable sign of each problem, the assumption it challenges and the cheapest credible test that could expose it. A risk register without an assigned next action or a stop/go criterion is not a useful project deliverable. Distinguish a design variation from an independent protection measure and seek qualified safety review where warranted.\n\n## Task D \u2014 Submit an engineering decision pack\n\nThe pack contains: (1) design-basis sheet; (2) flowsheet or concept comparison; (3) numbered stream and parameter tables with units; (4) independent hand checks; (5) controlled simulation figures and exported CSV; (6) constraints and exclusions; (7) cost or utility sensitivity where relevant; (8) failure and uncertainty register; (9) recommendation with what evidence would reverse it; (10) references and revision log. Document whether assumptions are invented for pedagogy, grounded in a published reference, requested from a supplier, or measured. A professionally reviewed design would need additional engineering discipline sign-offs.\n\n## Acceptance test\n\nA reviewer unfamiliar with your work should be able to reproduce a benchmark from the unit, locate the 3 related labs, see why the governing equations are dimensionally consistent, find at least one intentionally adverse case, and identify an unresolved assumption that could invalidate the proposed decision. The case is: A tank, transfer pump, process heater and heat-exchanger candidate are provisionally sized for the conceptual flowsheet. All evidence obtained and not obtained should be distinguishable. Do not present an unresolved conceptual model as an approved site or plant design.\n\n[Unit notes](../units/unit-04.html) \u00b7 [Unit assessment](../assessments/unit-04.html)"
